# drivers/vxtux/thermal/Kconfig — GPU power model from RE

config VXTUX_SPRD_GPU_COOLING
	tristate "Spreadtrum GPU devfreq cooling power model (RE)"
	depends on DEVFREQ_THERMAL
	help
	  Unisoc (SPRD) GPU power model for devfreq_cooling, reconstructed
	  from vendor blob sprd_gpu_cooling.ko (Android 13 / kernel 5.4.210).

	  Provides two exports identical to blob:
	    create_gpu_cooling_device()  -- called by GPU driver
	    destroy_gpu_cooling_device()
	  Model uses DT coefficients from node "sprd,mali-power-model"
	  (sprd,temp-scale / sprd,voltage-scale / sprd,core-base /
	   sprd,dynamic-core ...) and temperature from thermal zone "gpu-thmzone".

	  Bit-exact RE result (C == blob assembly) proven by
	  tools/re/re_gpu_cooling_model.py; board coefficients extracted from
	  blobs/fdt_live.dtb via tools/re/re_fdt_gpu_cooling.py.

	  This driver is ABI provider without in-tree consumer (Mali DDK that
	  calls it not in this tree). Default n.

config SPRD_TSENSOR_THERMAL
	tristate "Spreadtrum SC27xx/SC2730 PMIC temperature sensor bridge"
	depends on OF && REGMAP && MFD_SC27XX_PMIC
	help
	  Bridge the SC27xx/SC2730 PMIC on-chip temperature sensor ("tsensor",
	  base register 0x1b34 on SC2730) into the Linux thermal framework.

	  Ported from the vendor blob sc27xx_tsensor_thermal.ko (Android 13 /
	  kernel 5.4.210) via work/donors_20260930/bocchi_unisoc54. Register
	  access goes through the PMIC regmap handed down by
	  drivers/mfd/sprd-sc27xx-spi.c, the same way drivers/nvmem/
	  sc27xx-efuse.c does it.

	  TWO WARNINGS, both measured (docs/baterai_termal_bedah_20260930.md):
	    1. The driver only registers thermal zones when /chosen/bootargs
	       contains "androidboot.mode=cali". Otherwise probe returns 0 and
	       registers nothing, with a dev_warn. That is the vendor blob's own
	       behaviour, not a stub: this is a factory calibration path, not a
	       runtime thermal sensor.
	    2. The "OUT" channel (sensor id 1) interpolates a 256-entry v2t_table
	       whose first 136 entries are one flat plateau, so it is not
	       factory calibrated.

	  DTS shape required by the ported driver (OF thermal binding):
	    #thermal-sensor-cells = <0>; on the sensor node, plus a
	    thermal-zones node whose thermal-sensors = <&tsensor>;

	  Say Y if you need the SC2730 factory calibration path, otherwise say N.

config SPRD_SOC_THM
	tristate "Unisoc virtual thermal zone aggregator"
	depends on OF
	help
	  "virtual-thermal" platform driver: registers a thermal zone that
	  reports the MAXIMUM of several real thermal zones, listed by phandle
	  in the property "thmzone-cells" and resolved by node name. The zone
	  id comes from the "thm-sensor" OF alias.

	  Ported from the vendor blob sprd_soc_thm.ko (kernel 5.4.210, 3
	  functions, 960 bytes of live text) via
	  work/donors_20260930/bocchi_unisoc54. No MMIO, no interrupt, no
	  polling - a single thermal_zone_device_update() at probe.

	  Only useful together with SPRD_CPU_COOLING, whose power model is fed
	  by the aggregate. Say N if you do not use that.

config SPRD_CPU_COOLING
	tristate "Unisoc CPU cooling device with static power model"
	depends on OF && CPU_FREQ && PM_OPP
	help
	  CPU cooling device driven by a static power model (leakage plus a
	  temperature/voltage scale polynomial) rather than by cpufreq_cooling.
	  The state table is the cluster's PM OPP table; the cooling device is
	  registered with thermal_of_cooling_device_register() and consumed by
	  the power_allocator governor through get_requested_power /
	  state2power / power2state.

	  Ported from the vendor blob sprd_cpu_cooling.ko (kernel 5.4.210, 31
	  functions) via work/donors_20260930/bocchi_unisoc54. Coefficients
	  come from seventeen "sprd,*" properties on the children of the
	  /cooling-devices node, indexed by the "cooling-device" OF alias.

	  Two limitations you should know before enabling it:
	    - The blob exported cpu_isolate_funs() to receive two CPU-isolator
	      function pointers from the Unisoc hotplug driver. No such provider
	      exists in this tree, so the pointers stay NULL and the driver falls
	      back to real CPU hotplug (remove_cpu()/add_cpu()).
	    - Leakage trimming from eFuse block 15 (sprd,efuse-switch) is not
	      read: the Spreadtrum OTP reader is absent from this tree, so the
	      sprd,core-base / sprd,cluster-base values are used instead.

	  The driver hot-plugs CPUs offline to reach a cooling state, so it
	  needs a DT that actually supports CPU hotplug. Say N if unsure.
